<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maku</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maku is a town in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> province in the far north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, built in a narrow gorge beneath an overhanging cliff near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> and close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>. From the mid-18th century to the 1920s it was ruled by hereditary khans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayat-tribe">Bayat tribe</a>, who kept their position under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> as governors of a frontier district.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jahan Shah's sons rebelled in turn: Hasan Ali, imprisoned at <b>Maku</b>, rose in 1458; Pir Budaq, governor of Shiraz, sought independence, was spared at his mother's request and moved to Baghdad, rebelled again and was defeated in 1464 and executed on his father's orders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Maku</b>, a town built under a cliff in the far north-west of Iran near Mount Ararat, came under the hereditary khans of the Turkic Bayat clan after the death of Nader Shah in 1747.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maku">Khanate of Maku</a></li></ul></section>
